---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SatelliteSlider from '../components/SatelliteSlider.svelte';
import TeacherVoice from '../components/TeacherVoice.astro';
import EvidenceHunt from '../components/EvidenceHunt.svelte';
// Image Imports
import nightImg from '../assets/korea-night.jpg';
import mapImg from '../assets/korea-map.jpg';

const title = "The Unfinished War";
---

<Layout title={title}>
  <main class="max-w-5xl mx-auto py-20 px-6">
    <header class="mb-20 text-center">
      <h1 class="text-5xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-4">{title}</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-slate-600 max-w-2xl mx-auto italic">
        Why is one half of the peninsula glowing while the other is pitch black?
      </p>
    </header>

    <section 
      id="section-hook"
      class="mb-64 scroll-mt-24 relative" 
      data-insight="If you were looking at this from space, where would you think the border is? Why is one half glowing and the other almost pitch black?"
    >
      <div class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">The Line of Light</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-600">Use the slider to compare the 1953 armistice line with modern satellite imagery.</p>
      </div>

      <SatelliteSlider 
        client:load 
        nightImage={nightImg.src} 
        mapImage={mapImg.src} 
      />

      <div class="mt-16 flex flex-col items-center justify-center text-slate-400 animate-bounce">
        <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] mb-2 text-slate-500">
          Continue Investigation
        </span>
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-6 w-6 text-blue-500" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 14l-7 7m0 0l-7-7m7 7V3" />
        </svg>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section 
      id="section-origins"
      class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24" 
      data-insight="I emphasize that the Koreans didn't choose this division. After 35 years of Japanese occupation, their peninsula was split along the 38th parallel by two external superpowers."
    >
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">A Peninsula Divided</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">
            Following Japan's defeat in World War II in 1945, the Korean peninsula was freed from 35 years of colonial rule. However, liberation quickly turned into division. The United States and the Soviet Union divided the territory at the 38th Parallel.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 rounded-r-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-blue-900 mb-2">The Cold War Proxy</h3>
            <p class="text-blue-800 text-sm">
              The US became involved under the policy of <strong>Containment</strong>—the Truman Doctrine's goal to stop the global spread of communism.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-3xl shadow-sm border border-slate-200 text-center">
            <div class="aspect-square bg-slate-100 rounded-full mb-4 flex items-center justify-center overflow-hidden border-4 border-white shadow-inner">
               <span class="text-slate-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase">Rhee</span>
            </div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Syngman Rhee</h4>
            <p class="text-[10px] text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest mt-1">South Korea</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-blue-600 font-medium mt-3">Backed by UN & US</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-3xl shadow-sm border border-slate-200 text-center">
            <div class="aspect-square bg-slate-100 rounded-full mb-4 flex items-center justify-center overflow-hidden border-4 border-white shadow-inner">
               <span class="text-slate-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase">Kim</span>
            </div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Kim Il Sung</h4>
            <p class="text-[10px] text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest mt-1">North Korea</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-red-600 font-medium mt-3">Backed by China & USSR</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section 
      id="section-timeline"
      class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24" 
      data-insight="I use this breakdown to show students how wildly momentum swung in just one year. From being pinned at Pusan, to reaching the Chinese border, and being pushed back again—it shatters the misconception that wars are slow, predictable marches."
    >
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-12 text-center">The Course of the War</h2>
      <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto relative border-l-2 border-slate-200 ml-4 md:mx-auto">
        
        <div class="mb-12 ml-10 relative">
          <div class="absolute -left-[49px] top-1 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-red-500 ring-4 ring-white"></div>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-red-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Summer 1950</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">The Invasion & Pusan Perimeter</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 mt-3 leading-relaxed">North Korean forces launch a massive surprise invasion, rapidly overwhelming the South. US and UN forces are pushed all the way to the southeastern tip of the peninsula, desperately holding a small defensive line known as the Pusan Perimeter.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-12 ml-10 relative">
          <div class="absolute -left-[49px] top-1 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-blue-500 ring-4 ring-white"></div>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-blue-500 uppercase tracking-widest">September 1950</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">The Incheon Landing</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 mt-3 leading-relaxed">General Douglas MacArthur executes a daring amphibious assault behind enemy lines at Incheon. The brilliant maneuver cuts off North Korean supply lines, allowing UN forces to break out of Pusan, recapture Seoul, and push deep into North Korea toward the Yalu River.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-12 ml-10 relative">
          <div class="absolute -left-[49px] top-1 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-orange-600 ring-4 ring-white"></div>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-600 uppercase tracking-widest">Winter 1950</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">Chinese Entry & Chosin Reservoir</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 mt-3 leading-relaxed">Fearing a US presence on its border, China enters the war with a massive, stealthy offensive. In sub-zero temperatures at the Chosin Reservoir, surrounded UN forces must fight a brutal, fighting retreat to the coast to avoid annihilation.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-12 ml-10 relative">
          <div class="absolute -left-[49px] top-1 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-red-600 ring-4 ring-white"></div>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-red-600 uppercase tracking-widest">Early 1951</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">The Communist Counterattack</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 mt-3 leading-relaxed">The combined Chinese and North Korean forces push the UN army back across the 38th parallel. Seoul falls to communist forces once again in a devastating setback for the UN coalition.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-12 ml-10 relative">
          <div class="absolute -left-[49px] top-1 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-slate-400 ring-4 ring-white"></div>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest">1951 - 1953</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">The Grinding Stalemate</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 mt-3 leading-relaxed">UN forces regroup and push the lines back near the original 38th parallel. The war of massive movement devolves into brutal trench warfare. For two years, soldiers fight and die over small hills while peace negotiations continually stall.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <section 
      id="section-armistice"
      class="mb-40 scroll-mt-24" 
      data-insight="This is the most crucial vocabulary distinction of the entire unit. An armistice is a pause button, not a stop button. That distinction is the only way to understand why the DMZ exists today."
    >
      <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-3xl p-10 md:p-16 text-center shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden border-4 border-slate-800">
        <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-full h-2 bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-500 via-white to-red-500"></div>
        
        <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black mb-6 tracking-tight">July 27, 1953:<br/>The Armistice</h2>
        <p class="text-xl text-slate-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed mb-8">
          An agreement was signed to end the fighting. But it was <strong class="text-white border-b-2 border-red-500 pb-1">NOT a peace treaty</strong>. Both governments still consider themselves the legitimate ruler of all Korea.
        </p>
        
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-4 bg-white/10 px-6 py-3 rounded-full border border-white/20 shadow-inner">
          <span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-red-500 animate-pulse"></span>
          <span class="font-medium tracking-wide uppercase text-sm">The war technically continues today</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section 
      id="section-legacy"
      class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24" 
      data-insight="I want students to see how a single historical pivot point created two entirely distinct modern realities. Connecting the 1953 ceasefire to the tech, pop culture, and geopolitics they see on their phones today makes the history feel immediate and real."
    >
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4 text-center">One Peninsula, Two Worlds</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 text-center max-w-xl mx-auto mb-16">
        The signing of the armistice froze the conflict lines but set off two radically different trajectories for the people living on either side.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-12">
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-b from-blue-50/50 to-white p-8 rounded-3xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
          <div class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-12 h-12 bg-blue-500 text-white font-bold rounded-2xl mb-6 shadow-md shadow-blue-500/20">
            S
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">The Miracle on the Han River</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">
            Though left devastated and deeply impoverished after the war, South Korea underwent a massive economic transformation. Initially ruled by military dictators, the public successfully demanded free democratic elections in 1987.
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3 border-t border-slate-100 pt-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="text-blue-500 font-bold">✓</span>
              <span><strong>Global Tech Giant:</strong> Home to leading brands like Samsung and LG.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="text-blue-500 font-bold">✓</span>
              <span><strong>Cultural Exporter:</strong> Massive international influence driven by K-pop and media.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="text-blue-500 font-bold">✓</span>
              <span><strong>Democracy:</strong> A highly prosperous, free society built from the ground up.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gradient-to-b from-red-50/50 to-white p-8 rounded-3xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
          <div class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-12 h-12 bg-red-500 text-white font-bold rounded-2xl mb-6 shadow-md shadow-red-500/20">
            N
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4 font-black">The Isolated Dictatorship</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">
            North Korea built a highly centralized state ruled continuously by three generations of the Kim family. The regime fell severely behind economically after losing Soviet support, leading to widespread famine in the 1990s and strict controls on daily life.
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3 border-t border-slate-100 pt-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="text-red-500 font-bold">✕</span>
              <span><strong>Totalitarian Rule:</strong> Zero freedom of expression, information, or movement.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="text-red-500 font-bold">✕</span>
              <span><strong>Economic Isolation:</strong> Severe systemic crises and long-term infrastructure damage.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="text-red-500 font-bold">✕</span>
              <span><strong>Militarized Focus:</strong> Funnels scarce national resources into weapons instead of infrastructure.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 p-8 rounded-3xl max-w-3xl mx-auto flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-6 items-start">
        <div class="bg-orange-500 text-white font-bold px-3 py-1 rounded-md text-xs uppercase tracking-wider shrink-0 mt-1">
          Current Threat
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Why It Matters Right Now</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            Because the war never officially ended, the border remains highly volatile. To secure its regime and deter international intervention, North Korea poured its resources into a nuclear weapons program that began in the 1990s. With their first nuclear test detected in 2006, frequent missile launches over neighboring countries continue to pose one of the most significant security challenges to global peace today.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section 
      id="section-evidence"
      class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24" 
      data-insight="Instead of lecturing about the stark differences between the two nations, I have students actively sort and investigate the data points as evidence. This interactive dashboard forces them to connect modern geopolitical status directly back to the constraints of the 1953 pause button."
    >
      <div class="mb-12 text-center">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">The Legacy of the Pause Button</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-600 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          The 1953 armistice stopped full-scale combat but left the underlying political division completely unresolved. Explore the evidence cards below to discover how this frozen state of war shaped two completely opposite societies.
        </p>
      </div>

      <EvidenceHunt client:load />
    </section>

    <TeacherVoice />
  </main>
</Layout>